# Engineering invariants: execution authority, side effects, failure semantics, change cost

Status: in force on `v1/invariants-impl` (not yet released). This file is the map from each rule to the code that enforces it, the test that proves it, and why it exists. It is checked by `node scripts/check_invariants.mjs`, so a row cannot point at a file that moved, and a rule named in code or tests cannot be missing here. It replaces no other document: `docs/EXECUTION_CONVERGENCE.md` describes the spine, `docs/v1.2.0/SEMANTIC_AND_ARCHITECTURAL_INTEGRITY.md` the acceptance gates.

How to read a row: **Enforced in** is the authoritative boundary (where the rule is decided, not where it is described). **Proven by** is a test that fails if the rule is removed. **Why** is the incident or audit finding, so a later change can tell a deliberate rule from an accident.

## Rules

| ID | Invariant | Enforced in | Proven by | Why (provenance) |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| I-LIFE-1 | A lifecycle change is decided at the authoritative boundary, under the driver lock: `run` never overwrites an existing operation id; `resume` and `decide` re-read the record after the lock is held; a COMPLETE operation or unit never leaves COMPLETE; unknown statuses are rejected at load. | `cli/src/core/operation_runner.ts`, `cli/src/core/operation_schema.ts`, `cli/src/core/operation_runner_persistence.ts` | `cli/test/operation_entry_integrity.test.ts`, `cli/test/operation_schema.test.ts` | Audit 2026-11-17: `operation_cli.ts` loaded state before the lock was taken, so a stale snapshot could be written over a finished record; `run` with an existing id replaced its history. |
| I-EFFECT-1 | A mutating MCP `tools/call` over HTTP is delivered at most once per model request. Only a definitive "no such route" answer (404/405/501 from `/message`) may fall back to the legacy URL; any other failure is reported as unknown. | `cli/src/mcp.ts` | `cli/test/mcp_tool_call_delivery.test.ts` | Audit 2026-11-17: `sendRequest` re-POSTed the same payload after any fetch failure, including a reset or timeout after the server had received it. |
| I-EFFECT-2 | An unknown outcome (timeout, cancel, lost connection) is never reported as a confirmed failure. The receipt says OUTCOME UNKNOWN, and a mutating tool in that state counts as possibly mutated, so the unverified-change obligation is raised. | `cli/src/core/spine.ts`, `cli/src/tools.ts`, `cli/src/types.ts` | `cli/test/spine_unknown_outcome.test.ts` | Audit: a timed-out `write_file` produced `candidateMutated=false`, so the workspace could change with no verification owed. |
| I-EFFECT-3 | A call identical to one whose outcome was left unknown by a crash is not re-issued silently on resume, even in auto mode: it needs an explicit approval. The identity is a hash of the tool and canonical arguments written ahead of the call. | `cli/src/core/operation_identity.ts`, `cli/src/core/state.ts`, `cli/src/agent.ts` | `cli/test/resume_reissue_interrupted.test.ts`, `cli/test/fault_kill_mid_tool_call.test.ts` | Audit: recovery marked the operation interrupted but the model could re-issue the same effect on resume. |
